 ## Documentation
 
+### Local native Markdown textarea extension
+
+This Journal Tree build adds an opt-in boolean `markdown` prop to `<textarea>`.
+It uses CommonMark with GFM tables, strikethrough, task lists, and autolinks.
+Native text runs and per-line metrics render inline formatting, headings,
+quotes, lists, code, rules, links, escapes, and entities. Tables use aligned
+monospace grids and horizontal scrolling; images use native GPUI image elements.
+Formatting markers hide outside the active word or block and reappear when the
+cursor or selection enters it. A source/display map preserves original source
+bytes and translates caret, selection, and mouse positions.
+Plain textareas keep their existing behavior. Cursor navigation, selection,
+undo, clipboard operations and IME still use the existing native editor. The
+extension preserves composition underlines across formatting boundaries.
+Markdown mode fills the defined viewport height for native caret scrolling.
+In Markdown mode, Tab indents the current or selected lines with a literal tab,
+painted at four-column stops without rewriting source. Shift-Tab removes one
+tab or up to four legacy spaces. Tab-indented paragraphs and lists retain their
+Markdown formatting instead of becoming implicit code blocks. Fenced and
+space-indented code remain available. Enter at the end of a list item carries
+its marker and indentation forward (incrementing ordered markers and resetting
+task boxes). Enter on an empty item removes its marker and indentation.
+Shift-Enter inserts a plain newline. List continuation does not run in code.
+An unordered marker followed by a space or tab immediately renders as `•`, even
+while its item is active. Enter-generated continuations also render immediately.
+Bare markers remain literal until completed. The source character and its byte
+offsets remain intact for editing, deletion, clipboard operations, and undo.
+Inside lists, a lone indented dash is treated as an empty bullet rather than a
+setext underline, so unfinished items retain normal fonts and line heights.
+Multi-dash/equal-sign setext headings are preserved. A single dash completed
+with a space takes bullet precedence after a paragraph; an uncompleted dash
+outside a list can still serve as a setext underline.
+Typing `--` after prose text substitutes an em dash (`—`), with a separate Undo
+step restoring the two literal hyphens. Code spans/blocks, escaped hyphens,
+URLs, and Markdown separators remain literal. Line-leading hyphens are kept
+for rules and underlines. Existing source and multi-character inserts are not
+rewritten; composition and explicit range replacements do not trigger it.
+Fenced code uses its info-string language label with the existing cached Syntect
+highlighter and `theme.syntax` palette, including during editing. Supported
+language aliases match `<code>`: JS/JSX, TS/TSX, Rust, Python, Go, JSON/JSONC,
+Bash, TOML, Markdown, HTML, CSS, YAML, and C. Missing/unknown labels, inline code,
+space-indented code, or blocks beyond the syntax engine's 512 KiB limit stay
+plain. Colours do not change fonts, layout, selection, source, or IME underlines.
+Raw HTML remains literal text. Table and image blocks reopen as editable source. Cmd/Ctrl-click
+opens supported links. Relative image paths use the process working directory.
+`theme.metrics.mdEditorImageHeight` controls preview height (default 180px,
+bounded by editor width); `mdHeadingSizes` and `mdHeadingLineHeights` control
+the existing four heading metric tiers. Obsidian extensions are not included.
+
+The native addon also exposes `journalTreeMarkdownVersion()` returning `3`,
+which the local launcher checks before starting the app.
+
 See [AGENTS.md](https://github.com/remorses/gpuix/blob/main/AGENTS.md) for detailed architecture, communication flow, and contributing guide.
 
